\ EMMPTR.SEQ      Expanded memory POINTER support       by Tom Zimmer   F-PC native port

\ F-PC native port: there is no expanded memory (EMS). EMM-POINTER still
\ defines pointers and keeps the chain, so code that declares them loads,
\ but using one aborts with "No expanded memory available!" just as the
\ original did on a machine without EMS. Plain POINTERs (heap memory, no
\ 64k limit) are the replacement.

\- EMMHEAD VARIABLE EMMHEAD     \ expanded memory pointer chain
                                \ normally defined in UTILS.SEQ

0 EMMHEAD !                     \ end of chain is NULL

: EMM-POINTER   ( dbl_bytes | name -- )         \ make a pointer "name"
                ( -- paragraph )                \ it's location
                16384. DMAX                     \ at least 16k bytes
                2dup 65536. d> abort" EMM-POINTERS limited to 64k bytes"
 ( +0)          CREATE 0 ,                      \ initialize to unallocated
 ( +4)          HERE  EMMHEAD @ ,  EMMHEAD !    \ link into chain
 ( +8)          DROP ,                          \ lay in size in bytes
 ( +12)         -2 ,                            \ expanded memory handle
 ( +16)         0 ,                             \ emm pages
                DOES>
                TRUE abort" No expanded memory available!" ;

: EMM-POINTERINIT  ( -- )       \ initialize the EMM-POINTER chain
                defers initstuff
                emmhead @
                begin   ?dup
                while   dup cell- off           \ clear pointer to zero
                        -2 over 2 cells + !     \ emm handle to -2
                        @                       \ next pointer in chain
                repeat  ;

' EMM-POINTERINIT IS INITSTUFF

\ EMM-BYE-FUNC released the EMS pages at BYE; there are none to release.
